        <p class="mt-4 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Dizziness and weight change are also described in the
          <a href="/withdrawal/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">withdrawal section</a>.
          A symptom that follows this pattern but lasts longer is a reason to check in with a clinician, not a reason
          to conclude that something is permanently wrong.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">9. What actually makes withdrawal easier</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 mb-4">
          The evidence is clearest on two levers: medication that reduces withdrawal and craving, and behavioural
          support that changes the routines and situations around smoking. Everything else is a supplement to those
          two, not a replacement.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-slate-700">
                <th class={tableHead}>Support</th>
                <th class={tableHead}>What the evidence supports</th>
                <th class={tableHead}>Practical note</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Combination NRT</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  A background patch plus a faster-acting form is more effective than a single form, and is
                  recommended in UK guidance.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Arrange the combination with a pharmacist or clinician; do not improvise two products together.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Single NRT</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Any licensed form reduces withdrawal symptoms and improves quit rates compared with placebo.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>Use as directed on the product and in the plan you agreed.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Varenicline</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  A prescription medicine recommended by WHO and NICE; trials show reduced craving and withdrawal.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Requires a prescriber who can check suitability. See the
                  <a href="/medications/varenicline/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">varenicline page</a>.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Cytisine</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  A shorter prescription course recommended by WHO; in one large trial it performed comparably to
                  NRT.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Availability and licensing vary by country. See the
                  <a href="/medications/cytisine/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">cytisine page</a>.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Bupropion</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  A prescription antidepressant with good evidence for cessation, though generally less effective
                  than varenicline in comparisons.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Not suitable for everyone; a prescriber screens for contraindications.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Behavioural support</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Counselling, quitlines, groups, digital programmes and peer support all improve quit rates, alone
                  and alongside medication.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>The format matters less than actually using it consistently.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Movement</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Useful in the moment for restlessness and low mood; not a substitute for treatment.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>A short walk is often enough. It also changes the scene, which interrupts a cue.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Sleep and caffeine</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Caffeine has a stronger effect after quitting because smoking speeds its clearance; cutting back
                  can reduce jitteriness and improve sleep.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>Reduce caffeine after midday for the first month, then adjust.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>Detox products</td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  No established role. The body clears nicotine itself within days.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>Money spent here is better spent on proven support.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The table deliberately stops at the level of class and principle. It does not list doses, schedules or
          combinations, because those depend on the product, the country, your health and your other medicines.
          Those decisions belong with a pharmacist or prescriber, and the product label is the authority on how a
          licensed medicine is used. The
          <a href="/quit-smoking/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">complete guide to quitting</a>
          explains how to combine medication with a plan.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">10. Why your timeline may not match this page</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The single most useful thing to understand about withdrawal timing is that the variation is not noise
          around a fixed schedule — it is the normal range. The Hughes review notes substantial individual variation
          in both intensity and duration. Two people who smoked the same amount for the same number of years can have
          very different first weeks.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Several factors reliably shift the experience. Heavier dependence — more cigarettes per day, a long smoking
          history, smoking soon after waking — is associated with more intense withdrawal. A history of depression or
          anxiety is associated with a harder time, and with a higher chance that mood symptoms need attention.
          Alcohol, poor sleep and a high-stress period make everything feel worse. Living or working with people who
          smoke adds constant cue exposure that no medication addresses.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Using medication and behavioural support tends to reduce intensity, though it does not make withdrawal
          disappear. People who plan for a hard first week often describe it as more manageable than people who
          expected quitting to feel effortless; expectation does not change the physiology, but it changes how a bad
          afternoon is interpreted.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Pregnancy, some medicines and some health conditions change how quickly nicotine is cleared and how
          symptoms are experienced. If any of those apply, the timing in this page is a starting point for a
          conversation with your maternity team or clinician, not a schedule to measure yourself against. The
          <a href="/populations/pregnancy/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">pregnancy page</a>
          sets out the specific considerations.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Finally, previous attempts matter in a practical way. If you have quit before, you already know which day
          is your worst and which situation catches you. That knowledge is more valuable than any average, and it is
          worth writing down before the next attempt rather than rediscovering it in the middle of one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">11. When it is not withdrawal</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Most withdrawal symptoms are uncomfortable but not dangerous. A small number of symptoms are not typical
          withdrawal at all, and treating them as such delays assessment. Seek medical help promptly for any of the
          following:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          <li><strong class="text-heading">Chest pain, pressure or tightness</strong>, especially with sweating, nausea or pain spreading to the arm or jaw.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-heading">Severe or worsening breathlessness</strong>, or breathlessness at rest.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-heading">Coughing up blood</strong>, or a cough that keeps worsening over weeks rather than improving.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-heading">Fever, persistent vomiting, severe abdominal pain</strong> or symptoms of dehydration.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-heading">Fainting, a racing or irregular heartbeat</strong>, or sudden severe headache unlike any you have had.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-heading">Sudden weakness, numbness, confusion or difficulty speaking</strong> — treat as an emergency.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-heading">Persistent low mood, hopelessness or thoughts of self-harm</strong> — contact a health service or crisis line the same day.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Two less urgent patterns also deserve a conversation rather than endurance. Symptoms that continue to
          worsen after four weeks, and symptoms that are severe enough to stop you eating, sleeping or working, are
          not what the typical timeline predicts. Neither is a reason to resume smoking; both are reasons to ask for
          help. If you use NRT and develop persistent hiccups, jaw pain, heartburn or a bad taste, a pharmacist can
          usually suggest a different form rather than you abandoning the attempt.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">12. How to use a timeline without turning it into a deadline</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          A timeline is useful for preparation and dangerous as a scorecard. If you read it as a promise — "by day 28
          I will feel normal" — then day 29 becomes evidence of failure, even though the evidence says nothing of the
          kind. Use the dates to arrange support and to recognise what is expected, not to grade yourself.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          A more useful habit is to track three things once a week: how often cravings arrived, how well you slept,
          and how your mood compared with the week before. Three lines in a note are enough. They show direction when
          a single bad day feels like the whole picture, and they give a clinician something concrete if you do need
          to ask for help.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Expect the path to be uneven. Week three can be worse than week two. A stressful Thursday can feel like day
          two. A craving can arrive in month five with no warning. None of that means the quit has restarted from
          zero, and none of it requires a new decision — only the same response as before: let the urge peak, change
          what you are doing, and keep going.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The timeline ends; maintenance does not. The point of getting through withdrawal is not to reach a date but
          to build a life in which smoking is no longer the default response to a break, a meal, a stressor or a
          drink. The
          <a href="/cravings/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">cravings playbook</a>
          and the
          <a href="/relapse/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">relapse section</a>
          are the practical companions to this page once the acute phase is over.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          This page is a synthesis of public guidance and systematic reviews, not a report of a new study. The
          hierarchy is: current clinical guidelines (WHO 2024, USPSTF 2021, NICE NG209, the US Surgeon General
          report), then Cochrane reviews and the withdrawal time-course literature, then the site's own research
          records. It contains no dosing instructions, no individual treatment recommendation and no guarantee. No
          clinician has reviewed it. That is a statement about the review process, not about the sources: they are
          public and linked below so you can read them yourself. Corrections are handled under the
          <a href="/editorial-policy/" class="text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2">editorial policy</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

